{
  "$schema": "../gen/schemas/desktop-schema.json",
  "identifier": "desktop",
  "description": "Desktop-only grants for the Markdown folder pillar, which picks a file or folder and then scans, reads, writes and renames plaintext notes inside it. Separate from default.json because the pillar reports itself unavailable on iOS and Android, so those builds were carrying grants with no feature behind them. What stayed in default.json is what `saveBlob` needs on every platform: a Save As dialog, a write, and the stat that proves the bytes landed, which Android's create-document flow makes load-bearing rather than decorative. Every grant here is scoped to $HOME, which the picker widens at runtime to whatever the user actually chose and a double-clicked file widens through take_pending_opens. The deny list is the part $HOME/** cannot express, refused whatever the allow says, because a deny always wins: credential directories that no notes folder is, the places where a written file runs on its own or replaces the installed app (LaunchAgents, ~/Applications, ~/bin, which Ubuntu puts first on PATH, AppData with the Startup folder and the per-user install, the PowerShell profile folders), and the app's own data with the webview profile. Only $HOME paths, because default.json also reaches the phone builds, where a path that does not resolve fails the whole grant. A deny list cannot name every such place; it closes the ones a script in the window was shown to use. The four window grants size and place the file window, a Markdown file opened from the file manager while the app was closed (markdownFolder/fileWindow.ts). `plugins.fs.requireLiteralLeadingDot` is true, so $HOME/** no longer reaches a dot-path at all: a hidden directory is covered only by a pattern that spells the dot out. The folder picker is unaffected because it widens the runtime scope with the chosen path itself, whose own pattern carries the dot when the folder is a hidden one.",
